{"schema":"er9.native_endorsed_delivery.v1","stage_id":"I-06-LUNA-CLOCK-FRESH-R001-control-revision-a001","adopt_current":true,"artifacts":{"proposal.md":{"text_utf8":"# Proposal: reproducible notebook and data workspace\n\n**Stage:** revision; 2026-10-06. **Decision:** keep Jupyter `.ipynb` and Python 3 for exploratory analysis; add a local project record, declared DuckDB transforms/optional DuckDB SQL, immutable execution records, conservative invalidation, and bounded artifacts. Reference source datasets by default. Begin managed execution on Linux in a disposable rootless OCI worker with networking off. This is a practical architecture proposal, not a built product, qualified sandbox, tested package matrix, or 5 GB performance claim.\n\n## Evidence and scope\n\n**Source facts** below are tied to primary references S1–S16 in `sources.json`. **Inference** marks consequences drawn from them. **Product choice** marks the proposed behavior. All prototype validation below is **proposed/UNEXECUTED**, except the inherited synthetic candidate checks W1/W2 in `witnesses.json`. Jupyter and DuckDB are separate, independently useful projects: Jupyter supplies notebook document and kernel execution mechanisms; DuckDB supplies an embedded relational engine with Python and CLI access. Their provenance layer, shared data contract, cache, invalidation, worker and save protocol are application work. [S1–S8]\n\nThe current same-arm manifest lists a frozen research proposal and its three catalogs, but no critique artifact. I cannot claim to have reviewed or resolved an absent critique. I rechecked the primary notebook-format/client pages and the DuckDB issue/fix/test/release chain. I corrected mismatched proposal citations: regression test S14, release S15, OOM documentation S16. The source catalog remains the admitted same-arm bytes; its source IDs support the corrected citations. [S2,S9,S12–S16]\n\n## Component and support boundary\n\n- Use JupyterLab/Server, `nbformat`, `nbclient`, and one tested `ipykernel` Python 3 environment. `.ipynb` is a useful standard document interchange. `nbformat` describes cells, execution counts and stored outputs; metadata can be optional/ignored, so the file does not define runtime, dependencies or provenance. [S2]\n- Use `nbclient` for top-to-bottom replay in a new kernel. Its documented API has per-cell timeout, hooks and exception handling. A separate supervisor must provide overall wall/resource limits, progress persistence, cancellation escalation and atomic publication. A cell timeout is not a total run bound; exact Jupyter, Python, kernel and nbclient versions must be pinned and tested together. [S1]\n- Use DuckDB Python for declared table transforms and DuckDB CLI for colleague `.sql` files, on the same exact engine release, settings and extension set. Its lazy relational API and file scans are useful mechanisms; Python conversion/materialization or blocking operators can still need large memory. SQL is DuckDB dialect, not generic SQL portability or a new notebook kernel. [S3–S6,S16]\n- Declare Python packages in `pyproject.toml` and commit `uv.lock`. Record interpreter/platform and artifact identity. A Python lock does not pin the OS, native libraries, OCI runtime, current kernel state, clocks, randomness, external services or undeclared reads. [S8]\n- Initially manage execution only on a tested Linux rootless OCI setup. On other OSes allow static notebook and result inspection and folder exchange; disable managed execution pending platform-specific validation. Support only Python notebooks/transforms and DuckDB SQL. Unsupported kernels/dialects/connectors/extensions fail visibly or remain static. No credentials, private datasets, profiles, hosted service, scheduler, live collaboration or broad language support.\n\nThe exact compatible package/image matrix is unresolved. At implementation kickoff select a released DuckDB LTS patch, pin the Python package and CLI to that same build, lock all Python packages/artifact hashes and record the OCI image digest. Captured DuckDB docs identified 1.5 current and 1.4 LTS as of 2026-10-06; this is not a tested combination. [S3–S5,S8]\n\n## Project and workflow\n\nA portable ordinary folder contains notebooks/scripts, `pyproject.toml` and lock, `.repro/project.json`, and `.repro/runs/<id>/` sidecars. The versioned project record stores project ID/format version; notebook/transform paths; relative or explicitly external data references; parser options and contract versions/hashes; declared transform graph; environment and image fingerprints; result pointers and lock state. It excludes credentials and account profiles. Each successful run is an immutable record. Bundle export includes only files/data/results the user selects.\n\n1. Create a project or inspect an existing folder without moving files.\n2. Register CSV, NDJSON and Parquet by reference. Record source path, size, parser/engine, schema contract and content digest. Do not copy large files just to register them or mutate originals. Compute SHA-256 by cancellable streaming read; while pending expose only weak identity and withhold verified-current status.\n3. Preview with row and byte caps, explicit sample method and truncation notice; inferred schema is a sampled suggestion.\n4. Author standard notebooks/scripts or declared transform nodes with input/contract hashes, code, explicit parameters, engine settings, output schema and artifact destination. Free-form notebook code remains possible but has weaker known dependencies.\n5. Run selected interactive cells in a persistent confined kernel. Record cell identity/hash, session, event order/count, timestamps and status; show displayed order separately from actual execution order. Interactive output is `unverified` for clean replay because mutable state and undeclared dependencies are unknown.\n6. Request clean replay. Show chosen notebook, locked environment and source identity; start a fresh disposable kernel. Show queued/running cell, elapsed time, stdout/stderr and structured errors. Cancel by interrupt, brief grace period, then terminate worker. Failure, timeout, cancellation or worker death never publishes partial output as current.\n7. Compare code, contract, data, environment, output schema/content fingerprints, row/reject counts and bounded saved/current differences. Equal counts do not prove equal data; say when ordering is unspecified and whether output is truncated.\n8. Save manifest plus results, close/reopen elsewhere, resolve references, verify hashes and report missing data/runtime/lock mismatch. Permit static inspection if execution cannot be reproduced. Export selected artifacts and small manifest in a folder or portable bundle, no credentials.\n\n## Data contract and identity\n\nEach versioned contract records file format/parser options, field names/order as relevant, required/optional fields, logical/physical types, nullability, accepted null tokens, named conversion rules, timestamp format/timezone, and schema-evolution policy. Distinguish absent JSON keys from explicit `null` with a presence flag or equivalent. Preserve Unicode. CSV quoting, encoding, header and null behavior are explicit settings. A preview never proves full-file uniqueness, null proportion, type consistency or quality.\n\nDuckDB CSV detection samples rows (the captured 1.5 docs describe a default 20,480-row type sample), allows overrides, and documents seekable/non-seekable sampling differences and ambiguous dates. Treat inference as a hint; a full-conformance claim needs a full scan. Pin engine and parser settings. [S5]\n\nConversions are named/versioned. Bad casts, overflow, invalid timestamps, malformed records, unsupported encodings and disallowed schema drift produce visible structured errors with source identity, logical record ordinal, field/rule, error class and raw value/record when permitted. Default is fail-fast. Optional quarantine stores rejected records and reasons separately and reconciles input/accepted/rejected counts; never silently drop or coerce malformed data. Additive nullable columns are accepted only by an explicit policy and invalidate dependent outputs; breaking changes require review and a new contract version.\n\nUse logical row identity `(full source-byte digest, parser/contract version, logical record ordinal)`. For partitions add normalized relative filename and deterministic file enumeration; quoted multiline CSV ordinals are logical records, not physical lines. Business-key uniqueness requires a full check. Path/size/mtime alone is weak identity; never silently update it after bytes change and call old outputs current.\n\nSQL output order is unspecified without explicit total `ORDER BY`. Stable export order requires declared keys plus a unique tie-breaker such as source identity. Otherwise mark ordering unspecified and compare as an explicitly canonicalized multiset. DuckDB documents some order-preserving operations and exceptions, not universal order stability. [S6]\n\n## Run provenance, statuses and invalidation\n\nKeep separate: displayed cell order; actual ordered kernel execution events; mutable kernel/session state; and output bytes associated with a completed immutable run. Notebook `execution_count` helps show execution but cannot reconstruct hidden state or input dependencies. Saved notebook output alone is not proof of reproducibility. [S2]\n\nA run manifest records notebook and stable cell IDs, notebook/cell hashes, displayed positions, ordered events/counts, kernel/session or clean worker identity, timestamps, status, error/cancel state, package lock, interpreter/image/engine/settings fingerprints, explicit parameters, declared environment, source and contract hashes, MIME/sidecar output hashes, truncation and dependency declarations. Large results are checksummed Parquet/CSV artifacts with bounded notebook previews.\n\nStatuses: **current** only for output from a fully successful committed clean replay whose recorded code, declared input, contract and environment fingerprints still match. **stale** when a known dependency changed or saved run predates current inputs. **failed** for the latest attempt’s failure/cancel/timeout; keep old bytes historical and never substitute them as successful new results. **unverified** for imported outputs, interactive-kernel output, weak source identity, incomplete provenance or missing runtime. Use text and accessible names, not color alone; link errors to cell/field and announce progress without flooding.\n\nConservative invalidation: any cell-source edit invalidates clean outputs for that notebook; changed source bytes/contract invalidates declared dependent transforms and notebooks declaring that data; interpreter, lock, kernel, nbclient, DuckDB, image or settings change invalidates runs made with the old fingerprint. Restart invalidates that interactive session’s provenance. Failure/cancel/interrupted save never promotes old or partial outputs to current. Preserve historical runs. If dependencies are unknown, use `unverified`, not an invented graph. A later lineage view can explain declared dependencies without claiming to infer arbitrary Python.\n\n## Replay guarantee and limits\n\nA clean replay starts a new kernel from exact notebook bytes, parameters, locked environment/image and recorded source digests, and captures what the completed attempt produced. The product can guarantee that displayed current output is associated with that successful run and matching declared fingerprints. It cannot guarantee bitwise/semantic identity when code depends on clocks, random values, scheduling, external services, undeclared file/environment reads, unpinned system/native libraries, extensions or nondeterministic behavior. Network-disabled mode makes some dependencies fail but does not prove purity. Unknown saved output is unverified. [S1,S2,S8]\n\nJupyter Server access grants the ability to run arbitrary code; authentication tokens control access and are not OS confinement. [S7]\n\n## Scale, caching, saves and concurrency\n\nThe 8-core/16 GB envelope and 5 GB source are targets only. Prefer DuckDB scan/lazy paths and bounded preview (for example 1,000 rows/2 MiB), avoid unbounded `fetchall()` or Python materialization, and show truncation. Export large result to sidecar with bounded preview. Sorts, joins, windows, global aggregates, pivots and Python conversion may use large intermediate memory/temp storage. Enforce explicit budgets and actionable failure. DuckDB describes out-of-core paths and remaining OOM cases; it does not establish this workload’s capacity. [S4,S16]\n\nCache declared transforms only, keyed on source digest, contract/parser, code, parameters, engine/settings, output schema/format. Publish only after full successful validation; verify hash on reuse, invalidate on any key change, bound/expose storage. Do not cache arbitrary notebook cells with hidden state.\n\nWrite to a unique staging run directory; flush/checksum, atomically rename completed directory and update current pointer last; preserve prior run. On recovery incomplete staging is inspectable/discardable, never current. Atomicity/durability differ by OS/filesystem and require validation. Use one advisory project writer lock and expected-manifest hash; conflict means reload/copy, not overwrite. Other readers can be read-only. Network/sync multiwriter support remains disabled until tested.\n\n## Untrusted execution boundary\n\nTreat notebook code as arbitrary code. Do not use import blacklists, AST checks, or prompts as isolation. Initial managed execution runs as non-root in disposable Linux rootless OCI: no host home/credentials/runtime socket; read-only project/data; separate scratch/artifact output; no network by default; no extra capabilities; CPU/memory/PID/wall/disk/output limits. UI brokers document edits/commits. Validate artifact paths, symlinks and sizes on host; terminate descendants on cancel. Keep interactive code in this boundary. If worker unavailable, disable managed execution and retain static view; never silently execute trusted on host.\n\nThis is defense-in-depth, not a container security proof. Runtime packaging, kernel escape, symlink/path traversal, resource exhaustion, mounts, cleanup and macOS/Windows VM behavior are critical unresolved security dependencies. The usability cost is limited filesystem/network access and setup friction. Test threat model before supporting execution on another platform. [S7]\n\n## Failure chain and limits\n\nDuckDB issue #14177 reports valid CSV rows disappearing in 1.1.1 on a macOS ARM64 case with malformed newlines and `ignore_errors=True`; reporter says `ignore_errors=False` or `store_rejects` avoided loss in that case. This is a report, not all-input proof. Commit `123b82b9053c4843559035b6723c867b2618b2d9` removes an early return when errors are ignored so scanner still looks for a schema-fitting newline, and adds a multiline five-row fixture/test. The exact test path is absent at tag v1.1.1 and present at v1.1.2; its query enables verification, uses `buffer_size=80`, `ignore_errors=true`, and expects count 5. The v1.1.2 release lists PR #14184. This establishes report-to-change-to-test-source-to-release-line, not execution of SQLLogicTest, broad parser safety, every platform, or current-version behavior. [S9,S12–S15]\n\nProduct lesson: error-tolerant reading can hide record loss. Default to fail or explicit quarantine with accounting; rerun this fixture against selected build before enabling tolerance.\n\n## Opportunity and alternative\n\nOpportunity: a compact lineage view across declared dataset/contract/transform/output edges, explaining staleness and schema/reject-count changes. Leave arbitrary notebook edges unknown. A manifest and optional Parquet bundle makes exchange by ordinary folders practical.\n\nPlausible alternative: Jupyter/nbclient only, with author-managed data preparation. It is familiar and smaller but leaves schema, identity, invalidation and SQL sharing to individual notebooks; use it if environment replay is the only validated need. Polars lazy frames plus DuckDB SQL are another alternative only if a named workload benchmarks better; two engines complicate conversion, cache, ordering and result comparison. Reactive notebook formats may expose dependencies but alter authoring and interchange. `.ipynb` is a good starting document, not a runtime spec. [S2]\n\n## Proposed validation — UNEXECUTED\n\n1. Pin one Python/Jupyter/nbclient/ipykernel/DuckDB/OCI image combination; build cleanly and test notebook open/save, interactive execution, clean replay, timeout, errors, cancel, progress and exact Python/CLI engine identity.\n2. Synthetic CSV/NDJSON/Parquet fixtures: Unicode, absent vs null, empty vs null, mixed types, timezone timestamps, multiline CSV, duplicate keys, malformed records and schema drift. Check sampled/full-scan labels, conversions, identity, rejects reconcile, source immutability and invalidation.\n3. Execute displayed cells out of order; edit a cell/source/contract/lock, restart, fail, cancel and kill. Assert execution order is explicit and no historical/partial output is current. Compare schema/count/content and ordering semantics.\n4. Run v1.1.2 regression fixture on selected DuckDB release if license/source permits, plus malformed-newline variants under fail/quarantine modes; measure no unaccounted row loss.\n5. On actual 8-core/16 GB host benchmark generated 5 GB preview, projection/filter, export, sort, aggregate and join separately; collect time/RSS/temp-space and set workload-specific budgets. No measurement exists yet.\n6. Threat-test OCI mounts, symlink traversal, network isolation, resource caps, child cleanup/cancellation and output validation; separately qualify each declared OS/runtime.\n7. Fault-inject staging/rename/pointer updates; test recovery and two writers on named local/removable/network/sync filesystems; disable unproven cases.\n8. Reopen portable folder on clean machine with missing/changed data and runtime; check status and static result display. Validate keyboard/screen-reader states, visible focus, non-color status and restrained progress.\n\n## Dependencies and unsupported cases\n\nNo tested runtime matrix, end-to-end sandbox, filesystem durability profile, source-hash cost, 5 GB result or team workload benchmark exists. OCI rootless availability, confined interactive editing, exact engine parity between CLI/Python, and large-file hashing are critical dependencies. Until full hashes finish, output is weak/unverified. Unsupported formats/codecs, schema changes outside policy, other kernels/SQL dialects, remote connectors, resource-exceeding operations, platforms without tested worker, and untested network filesystems must fail clearly or support read-only inspection. Do not silently coerce/drop, mutate data, execute on host, or promise replay beyond recorded scope.\n\n## Primary sources\n\nS1 nbclient execution/timeout/hooks/errors: https://nbclient.readthedocs.io/en/latest/client.html. S2 nbformat 5.11 format: https://nbformat.readthedocs.io/en/latest/format_description.html. S3–S6 and S16 DuckDB Python, relational API, CSV inference, order preservation, OOM: https://duckdb.org/docs/current/clients/python/overview ; https://duckdb.org/docs/current/clients/python/relational_api ; https://duckdb.org/docs/current/data/csv/auto_detection ; https://duckdb.org/docs/current/sql/dialect/order_preservation ; https://duckdb.org/docs/current/guides/troubleshooting/oom_errors. S7 Jupyter Server security: https://jupyter-server.readthedocs.io/en/latest/operators/security.html. S8 uv lock docs: https://docs.astral.sh/uv/concepts/projects/layout/. S9/S12–S15 DuckDB issue, immutable fix commit, v1.1.1 test-path 404, v1.1.2 regression test, v1.1.2 release: https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/issues/14177 ; https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/commit/123b82b9053c4843559035b6723c867b2618b2d9.patch ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/duckdb/duckdb/v1.1.1/test/sql/copy/csv/auto/test_14177.test ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/duckdb/duckdb/v1.1.2/test/sql/copy/csv/auto/test_14177.test ; https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/releases/tag/v1.1.2. Exact capture hashes, IDs, locators and applicability are in the adopted source catalog.\n"},"sources.json":{"input_id":"I-06-LUNA-CLOCK-FRESH-R001-control-research-a001-sources-json"},"witnesses.json":{"input_id":"I-06-LUNA-CLOCK-FRESH-R001-control-research-a001-witnesses-json"},"leads.json":{"input_id":"I-06-LUNA-CLOCK-FRESH-R001-control-research-a001-leads-json"}}}